\begin_layout Section
The Quantum Liouville Equation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Equvialent to the time-dependent Schrödinger is the quantum Liouville equation,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{d\hat{\rho}}{dt}=-\frac{i}{\hbar}\left[\hat{H},\hat{\rho}\right],\label{eq:QuantumLiouville}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[\hat{A},\hat{B}\right]$
\end_inset

 is the commutator:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left[\hat{A},\hat{B}\right]=\hat{A}\hat{B}-\hat{B}\hat{A}\label{eq:Commutator}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Though Equation (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:QuantumLiouville"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) is equivalent to Equation (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:TDSE"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) as a description of a closed quantum system,
 our interest in Equation (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:QuantumLiouville"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) lies in the fact that it may be extended to model open systems.
\end_layout

